Part 1 -- Before you start
==========================

What is in the package
----------------------

The release archive fetchconfig-web-2.08.tar unpacks to a directory fetchconfig-
web-2.08/ containing:

  File: fetchconfig-web.cgi
  Purpose: The application: one self-contained Perl script, run under FastCGI or
           plain CGI.

  File: fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl
  Purpose: Creates the PostgreSQL role, database and tables; bootstraps the
           admin account; can write the config file.

  File: fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.51.sql
  Purpose: Standalone idempotent SQL to add the 1.51 audit-log tables to an
           existing database (PostgreSQL 8.2+).

  File: fetchconfig-web-clean-audit.pl
  Purpose: Standalone script to prune old audit-log rows (needs a privileged DB
           role; -h for help, -s to print the role SQL).

  File: LICENSE-ADDITIONS.md, LICENSE-ADDITIONS.html
  Purpose: Additional terms under GPLv3 Section 7 (EU/German liability
           adaptation).

  File: PRIVACY.md, PRIVACY.html
  Purpose: GDPR data-privacy notes for the operator (data controller).

  File: fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.50.sql
  Purpose: Idempotent SQL that upgrades an existing database to the 1.50 schema
           (per-site access).

  File: fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.60.sql
  Purpose: Idempotent SQL that upgrades an existing database to the 1.60 schema
           (users.email, login throttle, password-reset tokens; PostgreSQL
           8.2+).

  File: fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.62.sql
  Purpose: Idempotent SQL that upgrades an existing database to the 1.62 schema
           (users.download_full_config, the device_notes table; PostgreSQL
           8.2+).

  File: fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-2.08.sql
  Purpose: Idempotent SQL that upgrades an existing database to the 2.08 schema
           (users.read_debug; PostgreSQL 8.2+).

  File: fetchconfig-web-clean-resets.pl
  Purpose: Standalone script to prune spent password-reset tokens and stale
           login-throttle rows (-h for help, -s to print the role SQL).

  File: fetchconfig-web-install-template.pl
  Purpose: Root-side helper, run via sudo, that installs, creates, reverts,
           deletes or renames a template file (v1.3).

  File: imageconvert.pl, README.imageconvert.md (+ .html/README.imageconvert)
  Purpose: Optional helper for embedding a small image as a base64 Perl
           constant; its manual (Markdown master, with generated HTML and plain-
           text).

  File: images/back.jpg
  Purpose: Login-page backdrop image.

  File: favicon.ico
  Purpose: Browser tab/bookmark icon. Optional; copy to the web server's
           document root (see Step 4) when the application runs on its own vhost
           or dedicated server.

  File: help.html
  Purpose: The in-application Help page (served from the help tree,
           <WEB_DIR><HELP_BASE_URL>).

  File: README.md, fetchconfig-web-documentation.html, README
  Purpose: Full reference documentation (Markdown master, with generated HTML
           and plain text).

  File: INSTALL.md, INSTALL.html, INSTALL
  Purpose: This guide (Markdown master, with generated HTML and plain text).

  File: DEVELOPER.md, DEVELOPER.html, DEVELOPER
  Purpose: Developer/architecture documentation (Markdown master, with generated
           HTML and plain text).

  File: CHANGES.md, CHANGES, CHANGES.html
  Purpose: Change log (Markdown master; the plain-text and HTML are generated by
           render-doc.py).

  File: render-doc.py, render-doc.md
  Purpose: Build tool: renders each Markdown document to styled HTML and a
           plain-text (ASCII) version (Python 2.7/3).

  File: fetchconfig-web.css
  Purpose: Required stylesheet. Must be installed at
           <WEB_DIR><CSS_BASE_URL>/fetchconfig-web.css (Step 4) or every page is
           unstyled and a red banner is shown.

  File: LICENSE, LICENSE.md, LICENSE.html
  Purpose: GPL-3.0-or-later licence (the plain-text master plus the generated
           Markdown and HTML).

  File: Makefile.PL, cpanfile
  Purpose: A prove-based test harness, and the Perl dependency manifest used by
           cpanm --installdeps ..

  File: t/
  Purpose: The regression test suite.

Requirements
------------

- fetchconfig 9.72 or newer already installed and working. fetchconfig-web calls
  fetchconfig.pl and reads its device table, repository and template
  directories; it does not replace fetchconfig.
- Perl 5.10 or newer. Tested on Linux and AIX.
- PostgreSQL 9.2 or newer reachable from the web server. (The database upgrade
  script is written to be PostgreSQL 8.2-compatible, but 9.2+ is the supported
  baseline.)
- A web server that runs CGI scripts -- Apache with mod_cgi / ScriptAlias, or an
  equivalent. FastCGI (mod_fcgid) is the preferred way to run fetchconfig-web --
  the process is kept alive between requests, which is markedly faster; plain
  CGI is the fallback and needs no extra modules (see Step 7 -- Web server
  configuration).
- Read access for the web-server user to the fetchconfig device table and
  repository, and execute access to fetchconfig.pl.
- Optionally sudo, if you want the Backup Now button to run live backups (see
  Step 6).
- Optionally <FETCHCONFIG_PATH>/tools/generate_template.pl (ships with
  fetchconfig), readable and executable by the web-server user, if you want the
  Templates page's Upload HEX capture button to build a draft template from a
  record-session.pl capture. It is run directly (no sudo); without it, the rest
  of the template tools are unaffected.

    [CAUTION]
    Long backups and web server timeouts. While Backup Now or the Empty Backup
    Cleanup scan runs, fetchconfig-web sends a keepalive (an HTML comment) every
    10 s, so the web server's inactivity timeout (Apache Timeout, default 300 s)
    only needs to be longer than 10 s, and BACKUP_TIMEOUT may exceed it.
    BACKUP_TIMEOUT (default 120 s) must be higher than the longest backup run of
    any device in the device table (bounded by its timeout, fetch_timeout and
    banner_timeout options). A reverse proxy or load balancer in front of the
    web server must not buffer the response, otherwise its own timeout applies.
    Under mod_fcgid, FcgidBusyTimeout (default 300 s) caps the total request
    time and so must also exceed BACKUP_TIMEOUT; FcgidIOTimeout is covered by
    the keepalive.

    [IMPORTANT]
    Under mod_fcgid you must also raise FcgidMaxRequestLen. Its default is
    131072 bytes (128 KB), which is smaller than the uploads this application
    accepts: a 100 KB logo or template arrives as roughly 137 KB once multipart
    framing is added, and a HEX capture may be up to 1 MB. mod_fcgid rejects the
    request before the script runs, so the browser gets a bare 500 Internal
    Server Error -- the application cannot report anything, because it never
    sees the request. The error log shows mod_fcgid: HTTP request length ... (so
    far) exceeds MaxRequestLen (131072). Set FcgidMaxRequestLen 2097152 to match
    $CGI::POST_MAX -- see Step 7.3 -- Request size limits, which lists every
    limit in one place. Plain CGI (mod_cgi) is not affected.

    The upload forms also refuse an over-sized file in the browser before the
    request starts, so with FcgidMaxRequestLen set correctly an operator sees a
    clear message rather than a server error. That check is a convenience only
    -- the server enforces every cap regardless -- and it cannot help if
    FcgidMaxRequestLen is left below the application's limits, because then even
    an accepted file may exceed what mod_fcgid will carry. Closing the browser
    tab during a run terminates it (audited as ABORTED).

Perl modules
------------

Most of what the two scripts need ships with Perl itself. The table lists every
module used, so you can check an unfamiliar system in one pass.

Core modules (present on a standard Perl install on Linux and AIX): CGI::Cookie,
Digest::MD5, Time::HiRes, MIME::Base64, Fcntl, File::Path, File::Temp,
File::Copy, IPC::Open3, IO::Select, Symbol, Errno, Text::ParseWords, POSIX,
Time::Local. The helper scripts additionally use File::Basename and
Getopt::Long, and the test suite uses Test::More, FindBin, File::Spec and Cwd --
all core.

    [NOTE]
    "Core" means shipped with Perl, not already installed. RHEL and SLES split
    core Perl into many sub-packages, so on those systems POSIX, Time::Local,
    Digest::SHA and Net::SMTP come from perl-POSIX, perl-Time-Local, perl-
    Digest-SHA and perl-libnet -- present if you installed perl rather than only
    perl-interpreter.

Modules that may need installing. The minimum versions below are the versions
the code actually needs, chosen so that the stock packages of RHEL 9, SLES 15,
Ubuntu LTS and Debian all satisfy them -- no CPAN is required on a supported
distribution. See Installing newer modules from CPAN at the end of this section
if you nevertheless need a newer build.

  Module: CGI
  Minimum version: 4.31
  Why: Request/response handling. Removed from Perl core in 5.22, so modern
       distributions no longer include it. 4.31 is the floor for the -samesite
       cookie attribute the session cookie uses.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libcgi-pm-perl (main)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: perl-CGI (AppStream)
  SLES 15: perl-CGI (Desktop Applications Module)
  CPAN: cpan CGI

  Module: DBI
  Minimum version: 1.614
  Why: Database access layer. Only the standard DBI API is used; 1.614 is what
       DBD::Pg itself requires.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libdbi-perl (main)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: perl-DBI (AppStream)
  SLES 15: perl-DBI (Basesystem Module)
  CPAN: cpan DBI

  Module: DBD::Pg
  Minimum version: 3.0.0
  Why: PostgreSQL driver for DBI (loaded at runtime via the dbi:Pg: DSN). No
       pg_* driver attributes are used, so any 3.x works.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libdbd-pg-perl (universe)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: perl-DBD-Pg (AppStream)
  SLES 15: perl-DBD-Pg (Development Tools Module)
  CPAN: cpan DBD::Pg

  Module: Algorithm::Diff
  Minimum version: any
  Why: Side-by-side and unified config diffs.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libalgorithm-diff-perl (main)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: perl-Algorithm-Diff (AppStream)
  SLES 15: perl-Algorithm-Diff (SLE base)
  CPAN: cpan Algorithm::Diff

  Module: Filesys::Df
  Minimum version: any
  Why: Optional -- only for the Tools -> Disk space page (free-space figures).
       Loaded lazily; without it the app runs normally and that one tool shows
       an install hint.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libfilesys-df-perl (main)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: perl-Filesys-Df (EPEL 9 only)
  SLES 15: perl-Filesys-Df (PackageHub)
  CPAN: cpan Filesys::Df

  Module: FCGI
  Minimum version: 0.67 (tested 0.82)
  Why: Optional -- only to run under FastCGI / mod_fcgid (see Running under
       mod_fcgid). Loaded lazily; without it the script runs as plain CGI.
       CGI::Fast is not used and must not be installed in its place.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libfcgi-perl (main)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: perl-FCGI (AppStream)
  SLES 15: not packaged -- use cpan FCGI
  CPAN: cpan FCGI

  Module: Crypt::Bcrypt
  Minimum version: any
  Why: Optional but security-relevant -- enables bcrypt ($2b$) password hashing.
       When present, new and changed passwords are stored as bcrypt and legacy
       $apr1$ hashes are upgraded to bcrypt on the owner's next login. Without
       it, passwords fall back to the legacy $apr1$ format.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libcrypt-bcrypt-perl (universe)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: not packaged -- use CPAN
  SLES 15: not packaged -- use CPAN
  CPAN: cpan Crypt::Bcrypt

  Module: IO::Socket::SSL
  Minimum version: any
  Why: Optional -- gives Net::SMTP (core) STARTTLS/SSL for the password-reset
       email (EMAIL = 1). Without EMAIL = 1 nothing here is loaded, and the
       "Forgot password?" page just tells users to contact the admin.
       Net::SMTP::SSL is not used -- do not install it in place of this.
  Debian / Ubuntu: libio-socket-ssl-perl (main)
  RHEL 9 / Rocky 9: perl-IO-Socket-SSL (AppStream)
  SLES 15: perl-IO-Socket-SSL (SLE base)
  CPAN: cpan IO::Socket::SSL

    [IMPORTANT]
    SLES 15: perl-CGI lives in the Desktop Applications Module and perl-DBD-Pg
    in the Development Tools Module. Neither module is enabled on a default SLES
    install, so zypper install perl-CGI perl-DBD-Pg fails until you activate
    them, e.g. SUSEConnect -p sle-module-desktop-applications/15.6/x86_64 and
    SUSEConnect -p sle-module-development-tools/15.6/x86_64 (adjust the SP and
    architecture). perl-Filesys-Df additionally needs PackageHub.

    RHEL 9: every Perl package above is in AppStream (BaseOS ships none, and CRB
    has none of these). perl-Filesys-Df is in EPEL 9 only, so it needs dnf
    install epel-release first.

    Ubuntu: libdbd-pg-perl and libcrypt-bcrypt-perl are in universe, not main --
    relevant if your policy requires main-only packages.

Digest::SHA is not needed by the core app. This is worth knowing if you audit
dependencies: password hashes are pure-Perl Apache MD5 ($apr1$) computed with
Digest::MD5, SHA-256/512 ($5$/$6$) hashes are verified through the system
crypt(), and the device-table change-detection digest also uses Digest::MD5 (a
change detector, not a security primitive) -- so the script runs on older Perls
such as the one shipped with AIX. Digest::SHA (core since Perl 5.9.3) is loaded
lazily, only by two optional paths: the self-service password-reset feature
(EMAIL = 1) hashes its tokens with it (required if you enable that feature), and
the session-recorder download shows a SHA-256 beside each file only if the
module is available (the checksum is simply omitted otherwise). Neither affects
the rest of the app on a Perl without it.

Check what is already present, with the versions, in one command:

    perl -MCGI\ 4.31 -MDBI\ 1.614 -MDBD::Pg\ 3.0.0 -MAlgorithm::Diff \
         -MDigest::MD5 -MTime::HiRes -MPOSIX -e 'print "all modules found\n"'

Any module that is missing -- or too old -- is named in the error. To see what
you actually have:

    perl -MCGI -MDBI -MDBD::Pg -e \
      'printf "CGI %s\nDBI %s\nDBD::Pg %s\n", $CGI::VERSION, $DBI::VERSION, $DBD::Pg::VERSION'

Keeping modules patched
.......................

Two advisories are worth knowing. Neither is expressed as a minimum version in
Makefile.PL, deliberately: enterprise distributions backport security fixes
without changing the version string, so a high floor would reject a patched
vendor package while accepting an unpatched one built from CPAN.

- DBI before 1.650 -- a string assigned to the Profile attribute is interpolated
  into a string eval (CVE-2026-14380, CVSS 8.8). This application never sets
  Profile, uses a plain dbi:Pg: DSN with no driver-attribute clause, and under
  mod_cgi/mod_fcgid a remote client cannot set DBI_PROFILE -- so it is not
  reachable here. Still, run a patched DBI: either CPAN 1.650+, or a vendor
  package carrying the backport (RHEL 9 ships the fix in perl-DBI
  1.643-9.el9_8.5 and later, still reporting version 1.643).
- DBD::Pg exactly 3.21.0 -- an out-of-bounds write in quote_float()
  (CVE-2026-78183), introduced in 3.21.0 and fixed in 3.21.1. Older 3.x
  releases, including the 3.14/3.18 shipped by these distributions, are not
  affected. Avoid that single release.

Installing newer modules from CPAN
..................................

Only needed if your distribution's package is genuinely too old or you want a
patched build it does not carry yet. CGI and Algorithm::Diff are pure Perl; DBI
and DBD::Pg are XS and need a compiler plus the PostgreSQL client headers.

Build prerequisites:

    # RHEL 9 / Rocky 9 / Alma 9
    dnf install -y gcc make perl-devel perl-ExtUtils-MakeMaker libpq-devel
    dnf install -y epel-release && dnf install -y perl-App-cpanminus perl-local-lib

    # SLES 15 / openSUSE Leap 15   (cpanminus and local::lib come from PackageHub)
    zypper install -y gcc make perl perl-ExtUtils-MakeMaker postgresql-server-devel
    zypper search --provides /usr/include/libpq-fe.h    # confirm which pkg owns the header
    SUSEConnect -p PackageHub/15.6/x86_64
    zypper install -y perl-App-cpanminus perl-local-lib

    # Debian / Ubuntu
    apt-get install -y build-essential libpq-dev cpanminus liblocal-lib-perl

If cpanm is not packaged, bootstrap it (needs nothing but Perl):

    curl -L https://cpanmin.us -o /usr/local/bin/cpanm && chmod +x /usr/local/bin/cpanm

Option A -- private prefix (recommended). Leaves the vendor Perl untouched, so a
distribution update can never fight with your modules:

    PREFIX=/opt/fetchconfig-web/perl5
    cpanm -l "$PREFIX" --notest CGI DBI DBD::Pg Algorithm::Diff FCGI Crypt::Bcrypt

Use -l (--local-lib), not -L (--local-lib-contained): -l reuses the vendor
modules that are already good enough and installs only what is missing, while -L
ignores everything outside the prefix and rebuilds the whole tree.

Then point the application at the prefix. No code change is required -- the
script does not run under taint mode, so PERL5LIB from the web-server config is
enough:

    # mod_fcgid
    FcgidInitialEnv PERL5LIB /opt/fetchconfig-web/perl5/lib/perl5
    # mod_cgi / mod_cgid
    SetEnv PERL5LIB /opt/fetchconfig-web/perl5/lib/perl5

Alternatively uncomment and adjust the use lib line near the top of fetchconfig-
web.cgi (just above use CGI;). That form is the more robust one: it survives
suexec and environment stripping, and it is the only one that keeps working if
taint mode (-T) is ever enabled, since taint mode ignores PERL5LIB.

Option B -- install globally. Simpler, and fine on a host dedicated to this
application, but the modules land in the system Perl's site_perl and the
distribution's package manager does not know about them -- a later vendor update
of the same module can shadow or conflict with what you installed, and you own
the security updates for anything installed this way:

    cpanm --notest CGI DBI DBD::Pg Algorithm::Diff FCGI Crypt::Bcrypt
    # or, without cpanm:
    cpan -i CGI DBI DBD::Pg Algorithm::Diff FCGI Crypt::Bcrypt

Nothing further is needed: site_perl is already on the default @INC, so the CGI
picks the modules up with no configuration and no code change.

If pg_config is not on PATH (for example with PGDG packages on RHEL), point the
build at it before installing DBD::Pg:

    export PATH=/usr/pgsql-16/bin:$PATH      # or: export POSTGRES_HOME=/usr/pgsql-16

Verify which copy is actually being loaded:

    PERL5LIB=/opt/fetchconfig-web/perl5/lib/perl5 perl -MCGI -MDBI -MDBD::Pg -e \
      'printf "CGI %s\nDBI %s\nDBD::Pg %s\n", $CGI::VERSION, $DBI::VERSION, $DBD::Pg::VERSION'

Planning the layout
-------------------

Decide these paths before you start; they go into /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg in
Step 3. The defaults match a typical fetchconfig install.

  Setting: FETCHCONFIG_PATH
  Default: /usr/local/fetchconfig
  What it is: Where fetchconfig.pl lives.

  Setting: DEVICE_TABLE
  Default: /usr/local/fetchconfig/device_table
  What it is: fetchconfig's device table (read, and written by the editor).

  Setting: BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE
  Default: /usr/local/fetchconfig/backup
  What it is: Where the editor keeps timestamped .bak copies of the device
              table. Must be writable by the web user.

  Setting: SESSION_DIR
  Default: /var/lib/fetchconfig-web/sessions
  What it is: Login sessions (mode 0700, files 0600). Must be writable by the
              web user.

  Setting: BACKUP_TMP_DIR
  Default: same as SESSION_DIR
  What it is: Scratch space for Backup Now and the device-table writer lock.
              Must be writable by the web user.

  Setting: WEB_DIR
  Default: /www/virtual/fetchconfig/htdocs
  What it is: Filesystem directory holding the static assets (css, fonts,
              images, help tree); used for on-disk presence checks.

  Setting: BASE_URL, CSS_BASE_URL, FONT_BASE_URL, IMAGE_BASE_URL, HELP_BASE_URL
  Default: /, /css, /fonts, /images, /help
  What it is: URL paths under which the web server serves WEB_DIR and its sub-
              directories. HELP_BASE_URL is the help tree (help.html plus the
              scanned fetchconfig-web/ and fetchconfig/ menus).

You also need the identity of the web-server user (Apache's User directive, or
ps -ef while a request is running). On the reference deployment it is nobody; on
Debian/Ubuntu it is usually www-data, on RHEL apache. It is referred to as
<webuser> below.

Part 2 -- Fresh installation
============================

Step 1 -- Install the Perl modules
----------------------------------

Install the four non-core modules for your platform (see the table above), then
re-run the one-line check:

    # Debian / Ubuntu
    apt install libcgi-pm-perl libdbi-perl libdbd-pg-perl libalgorithm-diff-perl

    # RHEL / Rocky / Alma
    dnf install perl-CGI perl-DBI perl-DBD-Pg perl-Algorithm-Diff

    # SLES
    zypper install perl-CGI perl-DBI perl-DBD-Pg perl-Algorithm-Diff

    # Any platform, from CPAN
    cpan CGI DBI DBD::Pg Algorithm::Diff

    perl -MCGI -MDBI -MDBD::Pg -MAlgorithm::Diff -e 'print "ok\n"'

On AIX, prefer the IBM AIX Toolbox RPMs (perl-DBI, perl-DBD-Pg, perl-Algorithm-
Diff) where available; otherwise build from CPAN against the system Perl.

Step 2 -- Create the user database
----------------------------------

fetchconfig-web keeps its own accounts in PostgreSQL. The setup script creates
everything. Run it on a host that can reach the database server, as a user
allowed to connect as the PostgreSQL superuser (typically run it as postgres, or
pass connection details interactively):

    perl fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl

It walks through these steps, asking before each:

1. Create the role -- the database user the CGI connects as (default
   fetchconfig) and its password.
2. Create the database (default fetchconfig) owned by that role.
3. Create the tables -- users, audit_log, app_state, login_attempts,
   password_resets, device_notes, plus the 1.50 sites and user_sites tables for
   per-site device access, with the needed grants.
4. Import an existing htpasswd file (optional) -- hashes are copied verbatim, so
   existing passwords keep working. Imported users start with no edit or admin
   rights.
5. Bootstrap admin -- if no admin account exists it is created with the password
   fetchconfig. Change this immediately after first login (Step 8).
6. Write /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg (optional) -- writes a complete configuration
   file with the database credentials filled in. Say yes unless you prefer to
   write it by hand in Step 3.

The database connection parameters you choose here (DBhost, DBinst, DBuser,
DBpass) must match the configuration file.

Step 3 -- Write the configuration file
--------------------------------------

The CGI reads /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg once per request (the path is fixed in
the script). If the setup script wrote it for you, review it; otherwise create
it. The format is one KEY = value per line, # comments allowed.

A complete example with the defaults:

    # --- fetchconfig ---
    FETCHCONFIG_PATH        = /usr/local/fetchconfig
    FETCHCONFIG_BIN         = fetchconfig.pl
    DEVICE_TABLE            = /usr/local/fetchconfig/device_table
    BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE     = /usr/local/fetchconfig/backup
    FETCHCONFIG_LOG         = /usr/local/fetchconfig/fetchconfig.log

    # --- database ---
    DBhost                  = localhost
    DBinst                  = fetchconfig
    DBuser                  = fetchconfig
    DBpass                  = <the role password from Step 2>

    # --- sessions and scratch ---
    SESSION_DIR             = /var/lib/fetchconfig-web/sessions
    SESSION_TTL             = 28800
    SESSION_MAX_LIFETIME    = 86400
    BACKUP_TMP_DIR          = /var/lib/fetchconfig-web/sessions

    # --- Backup Now via sudo (see Step 6) ---
    USE_SUDO_FOR_BACKUP_NOW = 1
    SUDO_BIN                = /usr/bin/sudo
    # BACKUP_TIMEOUT must exceed the longest backup run of any device
    BACKUP_TIMEOUT          = 120

    # Cap on how many per-device sections the run-log viewer renders (0 = no cap)
    LOG_MAX_DEVICES         = 1000
    TEMPLATE_HELPER         = /usr/local/fetchconfig/fetchconfig-web-install-template.pl

    # --- accounts ---
    PROTECTED_USER          = admin
    MIN_PASSWORD_LENGTH     = 8
    MAX_PASSWORD_LENGTH     = 64
    # Optional password composition rules (0 = off). PASSWORD_MIN_CLASSES is the
    # number of the four character classes (upper, lower, digit, special) that must
    # be present. A new password must also differ from DEFAULT_PASSWORD and the
    # username.
    PASSWORD_MIN_UPPER      = 0
    PASSWORD_MIN_LOWER      = 0
    PASSWORD_MIN_DIGIT      = 0
    PASSWORD_MIN_SPECIAL    = 0
    PASSWORD_MIN_CLASSES    = 0
    DEFAULT_PASSWORD        = fetchconfig

    # --- email / password reset (optional) ---
    # EMAIL = 1 turns on the self-service "Forgot password?" flow (needs Net::SMTP
    # and the SMTP_* settings below). EMAIL = 0 (default) just tells users to
    # contact the admin. SMTP_SECURITY is starttls (default), ssl or none.
    # SMTP AUTH is used only when EMAIL_AUTH = 1.
    EMAIL                   = 0
    SMTP_HOST               = mail.example.com
    SMTP_PORT               = 587
    SMTP_SECURITY           = starttls
    EMAIL_AUTH              = 0
    #SMTP_USER              = fetchconfig-web
    #SMTP_PASS              = secret
    EMAIL_FROM              = fetchconfig-web@example.com
    # Public base URL (scheme + host[:port], no path) used to build the reset link.
    # REQUIRED when EMAIL = 1: the link is never derived from the request's Host
    # header (that would let an attacker redirect reset links to their own server),
    # so without SITE_URL no reset email is sent.
    SITE_URL                = https://fetchconfig.example.com

    # --- misc ---
    HTTPS_ENABLED           = 0
    SHOW_RENDER_TIME        = 0
    # Syntax highlighting for template-backed ("generic") devices whose template
    # does not declare a syntax_style. auto = detect from content (default);
    # none = plain text; or a scheme name (cisco-ios, json, xml, generic, ...).
    GENERIC_SYNTAX          = auto
    WEB_DIR                 = /www/virtual/fetchconfig/htdocs
    BASE_URL                = /
    CSS_BASE_URL            = /css
    IMAGE_BASE_URL          = /images
    FONT_BASE_URL           = /fonts
    HELP_BASE_URL           = /help
    # Overrides the version shown in the footer and About dialog. Omit it to show
    # the version the script ships as; the About dialog always shows that one too.
    #APP_VERSION            = 2.08
    COPYRIGHT               = 2026 (c) Your Organisation

Protect it: it contains the database password.

    chown <webuser> /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg   # the httpd run-time user
    chmod 0600 /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg

It must be readable by the web-server user and nobody else: the application
checks the mode on every request and shows a persistent warning while any group
or other permission bit is set, so a group-readable 640 will keep the banner up.
Every key is documented in the Configuration section of README.md.

Protect the configuration file. It contains DBpass and SMTP_PASS in cleartext.
It must be readable by the web-server user only (the application shows a warning
on every page until it is):

    chown nobody /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg      # the httpd run-time user (User directive)
    chmod 0600   /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg

Step 4 -- Install the CGI and static files
------------------------------------------

Copy the script into your CGI directory and make it executable:

    cp fetchconfig-web.cgi /www/cgi-bin/
    chmod 755 /www/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-web.cgi

Install the static files into WEB_DIR (the filesystem directory the web server
serves at BASE_URL). The stylesheet is required -- it must exist at
<WEB_DIR><CSS_BASE_URL>/fetchconfig-web.css, or every page is unstyled and a red
"directories not configured" banner is shown:

    WEB=/www/virtual/fetchconfig/htdocs
    mkdir -p $WEB/css $WEB/images
    cp fetchconfig-web.css $WEB/css/           # REQUIRED stylesheet
    cp images/back.jpg     $WEB/images/        # optional login backdrop

The help tree. The Help menu is served from the help tree,
<WEB_DIR><HELP_BASE_URL> (default .../htdocs/help). It holds help.html (the Help
page) directly, plus two sub-directories whose *.html files become the two
"additional help" menus:

    mkdir -p $WEB/help/fetchconfig-web $WEB/help/fetchconfig
    cp help.html $WEB/help/                     # the Help page

    # "fetchconfig-web - additional help": copy whichever documents you want listed.
    cp fetchconfig-web-documentation.html INSTALL.html CHANGES.html DEVELOPER.html \
       LICENSE.html LICENSE-ADDITIONS.html PRIVACY.html \
       $WEB/help/fetchconfig-web/

    # "fetchconfig - additional help": the fetchconfig (CLI) manuals, if you have
    # them rendered as HTML.
    cp fetchconfig-documentation.html How-To-add-a-model.html \
       README.generic-model.html README.record-session.html \
       README.template_engine.html \
       $WEB/help/fetchconfig/

Each menu entry's label comes from the file's <title>; entries are sorted by
label and open in a new tab. You curate each menu by file placement -- copy in
only the documents you want exposed. The two sub-directories are read-only to
the application (it lists and links the files, never writes them), so they need
no web-server write access. A sub-directory that is missing or holds no .html
shows a muted "No additional helpfiles available - see INSTALL.md"; a sub-
directory with more than fifteen .html files is treated as a misconfiguration
and shows a single "too many help documents in <dir>" placeholder.

The web server must serve $WEB at BASE_URL (e.g. the vhost DocumentRoot at /).
The backdrop image is optional (the login page shows a plain background without
it).

Session recorder download (optional). The Upload HEX capture block shows
download buttons for the session recorder only if the files are present in
WEB_DIR -- they are not shipped with fetchconfig-web. Build the Windows
executable from fetchconfig's contrib/windows directory and drop both it and the
Python source into WEB_DIR (served at BASE_URL):

    cp record_session.exe record_session.py $WEB/      # from fetchconfig contrib/windows

Each button appears only when its file exists; the whole row is hidden when
neither is present. The app shows a SHA-256 beside each download (computed once
and cached in SESSION_DIR, re-hashed only when the file's mtime/size changes --
no sidecar needed). record_session.py requires Python 3.12. The .exe is an
unsigned PyInstaller build, so Windows SmartScreen or antivirus may warn on
first run; the SHA-256 lets users verify it, and you can sign the executable if
you have a code-signing certificate.

Favicon (optional). favicon.ico is the icon browsers show on the tab and in
bookmarks. Browsers request it from the document root of the host
(/favicon.ico), so when fetchconfig-web runs on its own vhost or a dedicated web
server, copy it there:

    cp favicon.ico /www/virtual/fetchconfig/htdocs/   # the vhost's DocumentRoot

If the application is instead served from a sub-path of a shared server, place
favicon.ico at that server's document root (or leave it out -- a missing favicon
only means the browser shows its default tab icon). The Help page (help.html) is
read from the help tree (<WEB_DIR><HELP_BASE_URL>) on the filesystem and linked
at <BASE_URL><HELP_BASE_URL>. The two "additional help" menus list whatever
*.html documents you place in the fetchconfig-web/ and fetchconfig/ sub-
directories of the help tree, so a page you do not copy simply does not appear.

Install the root-side template helper next to fetchconfig so the template editor
can save files it cannot write directly (only needed if you use the template
editor):

    cp fetchconfig-web-install-template.pl /usr/local/fetchconfig/
    chmod 755 /usr/local/fetchconfig/fetchconfig-web-install-template.pl

The helper is v1.3. The subcommand set arrived in v1.2 (fetchconfig-web 2.05):
besides install and revert it supports install-new (used by template upload),
delete (used by template delete) and rename (used by template rename). v1.3 adds
no subcommand -- it refuses a .bak that is a symbolic link when reverting,
matching what delete and rename already did, so redeploying it is recommended
but a v1.2 helper remains functional. The single sudoers entry below (the script
path) already covers every subcommand -- no sudoers change is needed, just
redeploy the script.

The web server must be configured to execute .cgi in that directory and to serve
the static assets from WEB_DIR. That is Step 7, which gives the complete virtual
host in one place -- including the FastCGI setup and the request size limits.

Step 5 -- Directories and permissions
-------------------------------------

The web-server user needs to write to three places and read two more. Create the
writable directories by hand rather than letting the application do it -- the
application can only create a directory if its parent is web-writable, which the
fetchconfig tree should not be.

    # sessions + scratch (lock file, Backup Now temp tables)
    mkdir -p /var/lib/fetchconfig-web/sessions
    chown <webuser> /var/lib/fetchconfig-web/sessions
    chmod 700 /var/lib/fetchconfig-web/sessions

    # device-table backups written by the editor
    mkdir -p /usr/local/fetchconfig/backup
    chown <webuser> /usr/local/fetchconfig/backup
    chmod 700 /usr/local/fetchconfig/backup

    # the editor overwrites the device table IN PLACE, so the file itself must be
    # web-writable (the directory need not be)
    chown <fetchconfig_owner>:<webgroup> /usr/local/fetchconfig/device_table
    chmod 664 /usr/local/fetchconfig/device_table

Read access is needed to the repositories (every repository= in the device
table), the template directories and fetchconfig.pl itself; those keep their
normal ownership.

Why in place: the editor cannot use the usual atomic write-temp-then-rename,
because that would require creating files in the fetchconfig directory. It
therefore takes a timestamped .bak copy into BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE before every
write, so a failed write is always recoverable from the backup.

Writes are serialised: every save, bulk edit, restore and site-code rename takes
an exclusive lock on device_table.lock in BACKUP_TMP_DIR and re-checks that the
table has not changed (sub-second modification time, size and a content digest)
before writing. Two users saving at once can therefore not overwrite each other;
the second is told the table changed and nothing is written.

    A single empty device_table.lock file appears in BACKUP_TMP_DIR (or
    SESSION_DIR if that is unset). This is expected: it is a 0-byte flock
    target, created once and reused, mode 0600. Do not delete it while the
    application is running -- unlinking a lock file in use breaks the mutual
    exclusion. It is harmless to leave in place.

    Device-table backups are not pruned automatically. Every save, bulk edit,
    restore and site-code rename writes one timestamped .bak into
    BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE, so that directory grows over time. Prune it
    periodically -- either from the web UI (Tools -> Restore device table has a
    "delete old backups" control) or on the server with a cron job, e.g. find
    /usr/local/fetchconfig/backup -name '*.bak' -mtime +90 -delete. (These are
    the editor's own table backups, separate from fetchconfig's per-device
    config repository.)

    The report directory must be web-writable for logo upload. Tools -> Upload
    report logo writes image files into the report_dir configured on the email:
    line. Reports are already written there, so it is normally writable by the
    web-server user; if it is not, uploads/deletes fail with a clear message (no
    sudo is added for this).

    Session files are cleaned up automatically. Files in SESSION_DIR are expired
    on access, and a login runs a throttled sweep (at most once per SESSION_TTL)
    that removes any session past its idle timeout (SESSION_TTL) or its absolute
    cap (SESSION_MAX_LIFETIME after login), so they do not accumulate. No cron
    is needed for these.

Step 6 -- Backup Now and sudo
-----------------------------

The Backup Now button runs fetchconfig.pl for one device and writes the result
into the repository. Because the repository is normally owned by a privileged
account and not writable by the web user, the recommended setup is a narrow sudo
rule that lets only the web-server user run only fetchconfig.pl, as root,
without a password:

    # /etc/sudoers.d/fetchconfig-web   (install with visudo -f)
    <webuser> ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/fetchconfig/fetchconfig.pl
    <webuser> ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/fetchconfig/fetchconfig-web-install-template.pl

    [IMPORTANT]
    Both lines are needed if you use the template tools. The first is for Backup
    Now; the second lets the template editor and the Templates page write into
    the template directories through the privileged helper (TEMPLATE_HELPER,
    Step 4). Granting only the first leaves every template Save / Revert /
    Upload / Generate from HEX / Delete / Rename failing with a sudo error. Each
    rule names one script path and therefore covers all of that script's
    subcommands -- no sudoers change is needed when the helper gains one.

    Both are also gated on USE_SUDO_FOR_BACKUP_NOW = 1. That setting is not only
    about Backup Now: setting it to 0 also disables the template helper, after
    which template writes only work if the template directories are writable by
    the web user directly.

and keep USE_SUDO_FOR_BACKUP_NOW = 1 (the default). Backup Now then runs sudo -n
/usr/local/fetchconfig/fetchconfig.pl -devices=<tempfile>; -n means it fails
fast with sudo's own message rather than hanging if the rule does not match. New
backups are created with root ownership, consistent with cron-made ones.

Two things to weigh before granting this:

- It is passwordless root execution of that one binary for whatever runs as
  <webuser> -- in practice only this script, but it deserves your normal
  privilege-grant sign-off.
- nobody (and sometimes www-data) is a shared low-privilege account used by
  other daemons too. The rule applies to every process running as that user, not
  just this CGI. If that is broader than you like, run the web server under a
  dedicated user.

If you do not want sudo at all, set USE_SUDO_FOR_BACKUP_NOW = 0; Backup Now then
runs as the web user and needs the repository to be web-writable (see "Backup
Now and repository write permissions" in README.md for that option).

Step 7 -- Web server configuration
----------------------------------

This step covers TLS, the choice between FastCGI and plain CGI, the request size
limits, and the virtual host that ties them together. The complete configuration
appears once, written for Apache 2.4; the handful of lines that differ on Apache
2.2 are listed straight after it.

7.1 Serve over HTTPS
....................

Login sends a password and the session cookie is a bearer token; both should
travel encrypted. Terminate TLS in the web server in front of the CGI -- the
script does not do TLS itself -- and redirect HTTP to HTTPS so the login form is
never served in clear.

Then set in /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg:

    HTTPS_ENABLED = 1

This adds the Secure flag to the session cookie so the browser never sends it
over plain HTTP, and makes the application send a Strict-Transport-Security
header. Leave it 0 on an HTTP-only deployment -- with Secure set the browser
would withhold the cookie and login would appear to fail.

7.2 FastCGI or plain CGI
........................

As a plain CGI, Apache compiles the ~21,800-line script and opens fresh
PostgreSQL connections on every request. Under FastCGI the process is kept alive
between requests, so the script is compiled once per worker and connections are
reused -- noticeably faster on a busy server, and especially on AIX where
process start-up is costly. FastCGI is the recommended deployment.

The same script runs both ways with no code change: at start-up it does require
FCGI, and if the module is present and Apache started it through a FastCGI
process manager it runs a persistent accept loop, otherwise it serves one
request and exits. Check which mode is live in Help -> About (Run mode).

For FastCGI you need two things beyond the vhost:

1. The FCGI module (web-server Perl). CGI::Fast is not used:

    perl -MFCGI -e 'print "$FCGI::VERSION\n"'   # install libfcgi-perl / perl-FCGI if this fails

1. A socket directory. mod_fcgid keeps a Unix-domain socket per worker here. It
   must be on a local filesystem (never NFS -- sockets on NFS fail) and owned by
   the user Apache runs its children as (the User directive; often nobody,
   apache, www or daemon -- check with grep '^User' <httpd.conf>). On a
   persistent local filesystem it survives reboot, so it does not need
   recreating at boot -- unlike a volatile path such as /var/run, which does not
   exist on AIX in the Linux sense anyway:

    mkdir -p /www/mod_fcgid
    chown nobody /www/mod_fcgid      # the owner of the httpd child processes
    chmod 755  /www/mod_fcgid

Sockets appear here while a worker is alive and are removed when it exits,    so
an idle server may show none.

7.3 Request size limits
.......................

Three limits apply to every POST, in this order. They must be set so the
application's own limit is the smallest, otherwise a request is killed before
the script runs and the browser gets a bare 500 Internal Server Error with no
explanation -- the application never sees it and cannot report anything.

  Limit: FcgidMaxRequestLen
  Where: the vhost, in <IfModule mod_fcgid.c>
  Set to: 2097152
  Applies to: mod_fcgid only

  Limit: LimitRequestBody
  Where: Apache vhost or <Directory>
  Set to: leave unset (optional)
  Applies to: mod_cgi and mod_fcgid

  Limit: $CGI::POST_MAX
  Where: fetchconfig-web.cgi, near the top
  Set to: 2  1024  1024 (shipped)
  Applies to: always

In practice only FcgidMaxRequestLen needs setting -- its default, 131072 (128
KB), is below $CGI::POST_MAX. It is in the vhost below. LimitRequestBody's
default is already far above (1 GiB on httpd 2.4.54+; unlimited -- the literal
default 0 -- on 2.4.53 and on the whole 2.2 branch), so it never fires here; set
it only if you want the web server to refuse earlier, and then keep it at or
above $CGI::POST_MAX.

It is not only uploads that need the headroom:

Request                                    Typical size
-----------------------------------------  ------------------------------
Logo / template upload (100 KB cap)        ~137 KB with multipart framing
HEX capture upload (1 MB cap)              ~1.05 MB
Device-table save, 1000 devices collapsed  ~34 KB
Device-table save, 1000 devices expanded   ~286 KB
Device-table save, 5000 devices collapsed  ~178 KB

The device-table figures assume 2.07 or newer, where the editor and bulk-edit
forms are url-encoded. Before 2.07 they were multipart, roughly 4-6x larger, so
a table of more than about 670 devices could not be saved at all under the 128
KB default.

    [NOTE]
    The editor estimates the size of a device-table save in the browser and
    refuses to send one that would exceed $CGI::POST_MAX, so an operator gets a
    clear message instead of losing their edits. That check cannot help if
    FcgidMaxRequestLen is left below $CGI::POST_MAX, because then mod_fcgid
    rejects requests the application considers perfectly legal.

7.4 The virtual host
....................

One complete vhost, Apache 2.4 syntax. It assumes the defaults used throughout
this guide: WEB_DIR /www/virtual/fetchconfig/htdocs serving /css, /fonts,
/images and /help, and the script at /cgi-bin/fetchconfig-fcgi/fetchconfig-
web.cgi. Adjust paths, ServerName and certificate files.

    # Without this LoadModule the whole <IfModule mod_fcgid.c> block is silently
    # skipped -- that guard is why a missing module raises no error.
    LoadModule fcgid_module modules/mod_fcgid.so

    <VirtualHost *:80>
        ServerName fetchconfig.example.com
        Redirect permanent / https://fetchconfig.example.com/
    </VirtualHost>

    <VirtualHost *:443>
        ServerName   fetchconfig.example.com
        DocumentRoot "/www/virtual/fetchconfig/htdocs"

        SSLEngine on
        SSLProtocol           -all +TLSv1.2 +TLSv1.3
        SSLCertificateFile    /etc/ssl/certs/fetchconfig-fullchain.crt
        SSLCertificateKeyFile /etc/ssl/private/fetchconfig.key

        # Static assets: css, fonts, images and the help tree live under WEB_DIR.
        <Directory "/www/virtual/fetchconfig/htdocs">
            Options -Indexes
            AllowOverride None
            Require all granted
        </Directory>

        <IfModule mod_fcgid.c>
            # Socket directory from 7.2 (local disk, owned by the httpd user).
            FcgidIPCDir             /www/mod_fcgid

            # Shared process table. Set this EXPLICITLY (a file inside the IPC dir
            # is fine). On AIX especially, leaving it unset can make mod_fcgid fall
            # back to a SysV-semaphore lock the kernel rejects at startup with
            # "(22)Invalid argument: mod_fcgid: can't lock process table" (APR on a
            # 32-bit AIX build defaults to APR_USE_SYSVSEM_SERIALIZE); pointing it
            # at a real file on local disk uses a file lock instead.
            FcgidProcessTableFile   /www/mod_fcgid/fcgid_shm

            # Process pool. FcgidMaxProcessesPerClass MUST be >= MAX_PARALLEL_SCAN:
            # the scan tools open up to that many concurrent per-device requests,
            # and if the worker class is smaller they queue behind busy workers and
            # the progress bar appears stuck at 0. 8 covers the default
            # MAX_PARALLEL_SCAN (<= 5) plus the browser's own per-host connection
            # limit, with headroom.
            FcgidMaxProcesses         32
            FcgidMinProcessesPerClass  2
            FcgidMaxProcessesPerClass  8
            FcgidIdleTimeout         300
            FcgidProcessLifeTime    3600

            # Both MUST exceed BACKUP_TIMEOUT. FcgidBusyTimeout caps TOTAL request
            # time, so a long Backup Now is killed if it is lower than
            # BACKUP_TIMEOUT; FcgidIOTimeout is an inactivity timeout, covered by
            # the keepalive (every 10 s), but keep it above BACKUP_TIMEOUT too.
            FcgidBusyTimeout         650
            FcgidIOTimeout           650

            # REQUIRED -- the 131072 default is below what the application accepts
            # (uploads AND large device-table saves); see 7.3.
            FcgidMaxRequestLen   2097152

            # Only if the Perl modules are not in the httpd user's @INC:
            # FcgidInitialEnv PERL5LIB /usr/local/perl_lib
            # FcgidInitialEnv TZ       CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3
        </IfModule>

        # Alias, not ScriptAlias: with Alias nothing in the directory executes
        # unless the <Files> block says so, so a stray script is served static
        # rather than run. ScriptAlias would make EVERY file there a CGI.
        Alias /cgi-bin/fetchconfig-fcgi/ "/www/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-fcgi/"
        <Directory "/www/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-fcgi">
            AllowOverride None
            Options +ExecCGI
            Require all granted
            <Files "fetchconfig-web.cgi">
                SetHandler fcgid-script
            </Files>
        </Directory>

        # Password-reset links carry a one-time token in the query string; keep it
        # out of the access log (it would otherwise be readable by anyone who can
        # read the log, for up to 30 minutes).
        SetEnvIf Request_URI "action=reset_token" dontlog
        ErrorLog  /var/log/apache2/fetchconfig_error.log
        CustomLog /var/log/apache2/fetchconfig_access.log combined env=!dontlog
    </VirtualHost>

SetHandler fcgid-script makes only the named file run under FastCGI; add a
further <Files "name.cgi"> block for each additional FastCGI responder. Only a
script that runs a FastCGI accept loop belongs in such a block -- a plain CGI
handed to fcgid-script hangs until FcgidBusyTimeout.

Two directives belong in the server configuration, outside any vhost:

    # Do not advertise exact component versions.
    ServerTokens Prod
    ServerSignature Off

7.5 Apache 2.2: what changes
............................

Everything above is valid on Apache 2.2 except these lines. Nothing else differs
-- do not keep a second copy of the vhost.

  In the vhost: Access control, in both <Directory> blocks
  Apache 2.4 (above): Require all granted
  Apache 2.2 instead: Order allow,deny<br>Allow from all

  In the vhost: TLS versions
  Apache 2.4 (above): SSLProtocol -all +TLSv1.2 +TLSv1.3
  Apache 2.2 instead: SSLProtocol all -SSLv2 -SSLv3

  In the vhost: Certificate chain
  Apache 2.4 (above): chain included in SSLCertificateFile
  Apache 2.2 instead: add SSLCertificateChainFile
                      /etc/ssl/certs/intermediate.crt

Require all granted is 2.4-only: on 2.2 Apache refuses to start with Invalid
command 'Require'. The 2.2 form still works on 2.4 only if mod_access_compat is
loaded, and is deprecated there -- so use the form that matches your server
rather than trying to write one that suits both.

7.6 Plain CGI instead of FastCGI
................................

Drop the LoadModule, the <IfModule mod_fcgid.c> block and the <Files> block, and
replace the Alias with a ScriptAlias:

    ScriptAlias /cgi-bin/ "/www/cgi-bin/"
    <Directory "/www/cgi-bin">
        Options +ExecCGI
        AddHandler cgi-script .cgi
        Require all granted
    </Directory>

Nothing in the application changes -- it detects the mode at start-up. But
FcgidMaxRequestLen no longer applies, so $CGI::POST_MAX becomes the only
request-size limit, and the socket directory from 7.2 is not needed.

7.7 Activate and verify
.......................

    apachectl configtest && apachectl restart

Then load a page, reload it a few times, and confirm the worker keeps the same
PID across requests:

    ps -ef | grep '[f]etchconfig-web.cgi'

A changing PID means it is still running as plain CGI -- the handler did not
take effect. Help -> About shows Run mode: FCGI when FastCGI is live.

Reloading after an upgrade. Under FastCGI the workers are long-lived and hold
the compiled script in memory, so after copying a new fetchconfig-web.cgi the
running workers keep serving the OLD code until they are recycled. mod_fcgid
does not watch the file's modification time, so touch-ing the script does
nothing. Reload with a graceful restart, which lets in-flight requests finish
and replaces the workers:

    apachectl graceful

(apachectl restart works too but drops in-flight requests.) Plain CGI needs none
of this -- every request is a fresh process, so a new script file takes effect
on the next request.

Step 8 -- First login
---------------------

Browse to https://yourhost/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-web.cgi and log in as admin with
the password fetchconfig (or the one you set in Step 2).

Immediately:

1. Change the admin password -- User -> Change your password. Until you do, a
   warning banner is shown on every page.
2. Create your sites -- User -> Sites. Every device must carry a site= tag
   before the device table can be saved, so define the site codes first.
3. Create users and assign their sites and rights -- User -> Add user, then
   Users -> Sites, or Users -> (click a user) -> Edit site access. A user needs
   the Edit device right to use the editor and Admin functions for Tools, sites
   and user management.
4. Tag your devices -- Setup devices -> Edit device table. Untagged devices are
   shown with a red site placeholder; Tools -> Check site assignment lists any
   that remain untagged.

Part 3 -- Upgrading
===================

Upgrading the script
--------------------

Upgrading the application itself is a file copy; no data migration is involved
for the script:

    cp /www/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-web.cgi /www/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-web.cgi.old
    cp fetchconfig-web.cgi /www/cgi-bin/
    chmod 755 /www/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-web.cgi
    cp fetchconfig-web.css /www/pub/fetchconfig-web/css/     # refresh the stylesheet
    cp help.html /www/pub/fetchconfig-web/help/
    # Refresh the Help-menu documentation pages as well (see Step 4):
    cp fetchconfig-web-documentation.html DEVELOPER.html CHANGES.html INSTALL.html \
       LICENSE.html LICENSE-ADDITIONS.html PRIVACY.html \
       /www/pub/fetchconfig-web/help/fetchconfig-web/

    Upgrading from 2.02 or earlier: 2.03 moves the help files into a help tree.
    Add HELP_BASE_URL = /help to /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg (it defaults to /help
    if omitted), create <WEB_DIR>/help/fetchconfig-web/ and
    <WEB_DIR>/help/fetchconfig/, move help.html into <WEB_DIR>/help/, and copy
    the documentation pages into those two sub-directories (see Step 4). The old
    flat Help-menu list (fixed document names in WEB_DIR) is gone; the menu now
    lists whatever you place in the two sub-directories.

    Upgrading to 2.05 (template upload/delete): redeploy fetchconfig-web-
    install-template.pl (now v1.2 -- adds install-new, delete and rename). No
    sudoers change is needed (the existing entry is the script path). Template
    upload/delete use the same TEMPLATE_HELPER as Save/Revert, or write directly
    when the template directory is web-writable. No config or database change is
    required.

Then hard-reload the browser (Ctrl-F5) so cached JavaScript and CSS are
refreshed -- several 1.50 changes are client-side, and a stale cache is the
usual reason a fix "does not appear".

Running under FastCGI? Copying the new script is not enough -- the long-lived
mod_fcgid workers keep running the old code until recycled, and touch does not
trigger a reload. Run apachectl graceful after the copy (see Step 7 -- Web
server configuration). Plain CGI needs no such step.

Upgrading to 2.08
-----------------

    Order matters: run the database upgrade BEFORE copying the new fetchconfig-
    web.cgi into place.

    2.08 reads the new users.read_debug column by name in every user lookup,
    including the one that authenticates a login. Deploy the .cgi against a
    database that still lacks the column and no one can log in -- not even the
    built-in admin account -- until the SQL is run. The reverse order is
    harmless: the column is simply unused by 2.07, so the database may be
    upgraded minutes or days ahead of the code.

    The same applies to the other upgrade scripts: schema first, then the code.

So, in order:

1. Run the database upgrade (adds users.read_debug). Idempotent, PostgreSQL
   8.2-compatible:

    psql -U fetchconfig -d fetchconfig -f fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-2.08.sql

1. Copy the new fetchconfig-web.cgi, fetchconfig-web.css and help.html into
   place. Under FastCGI, follow with apachectl graceful as described at the top
   of Part 3 -- Upgrading, and hard-reload the browser so the new CSS is picked
   up.

Then:

- Redeploy the privileged helper (fetchconfig-web-install-template.pl, now
  v1.5). It gains the read-debug and delete-debug subcommands, which is how the
  viewer reaches a debug log that root created mode 0600. The sudoers rule is
  unchanged -- the one you already have for the template editor covers it. Set
  TEMPLATE_HELPER in fetchconfig-web.cfg if it is not set yet.
- Grant the new View device debug logs right (User -> Users) only where it is
  genuinely needed: a debug log is a full session transcript and can contain the
  device password in clear text, so the right is closer in weight to handing out
  device credentials than to a read-only view. Unlike the other per-user rights
  it is not implied by being site-unrestricted -- it must be granted explicitly,
  or the user must be an administrator. Downloading and deleting debug logs stay
  administrator-only.

Upgrading to 1.62
-----------------

- Run the database upgrade (adds users.download_full_config and the device_notes
  table). Idempotent, PostgreSQL 8.2-compatible:

    psql -U fetchconfig -d fetchconfig -f fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.62.sql

- Grant the new Full config right (User -> Users) to any site-limited user who
  should be able to download a device config or copy it unmasked; unrestricted
  users always may. Without it the config view, and its Copy button, are masked
  and there is no Download button.
- Device notes need no configuration; all users can add them (honouring site
  access) via the paper-note icon, Device -> Notes, and Tools -> Orphaned notes
  cleanup.

Upgrading to 1.61
-----------------

- chmod 0600 /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg (owned by the web-server user); the
  application warns on every page until the mode is correct.
- If you use the email password reset (EMAIL = 1): add SITE_URL to the config
  file. Reset links are no longer built from the request's Host header; without
  SITE_URL no reset email is sent.
- Reset tokens are now stored as SHA-256; any token issued before the upgrade
  becomes invalid (they expire within 30 minutes anyway).
- Add the access-log exclusion and ServerTokens Prod from Step 7.
- No database change.

Upgrading to 1.60
-----------------

- Remove REPOSITORY from /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg. The repository now always
  comes from the device table (device repository= or the model's default: line),
  exactly as for fetchconfig.pl itself. A leftover REPOSITORY line is ignored
  and shown as invalid in Tools -> Show fetchconfig-web.cfg.
- If the device table sets no repository= at all, every page shows FATAL ERROR:
  No repository found ... -- add a default: line with repository= for each
  model.
- Before 1.60, Backup Now stored backups of devices whose repository comes from
  a model default: line in REPOSITORY instead, if the two differed. Check
  REPOSITORY for such device directories and move them into the device's real
  repository.
- Run the database upgrade (adds users.email, login_attempts and
  password_resets). It is idempotent and PostgreSQL 8.2-compatible:

    psql -U fetchconfig -d fetchconfig -f fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.60.sql

- Optional modules. Install Crypt::Bcrypt to have passwords stored as bcrypt and
  legacy $apr1$ hashes upgraded on next login. Install Net::SMTP (3.x, with
  IO::Socket::SSL) only if you enable the password-reset email.
- Optional email reset. To let users reset their own password, set EMAIL = 1 and
  the SMTP_ / EMAIL_FROM keys in /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg (see the email section
  of the sample config), and set each user's email address in User -> Users.
  With EMAIL = 0 the "Forgot password?" link simply tells users to contact an
  administrator, who resets the password from User -> Users*.
- Housekeeping. Prune spent reset tokens and stale throttle rows periodically
  (e.g. from cron) with fetchconfig-web-clean-resets.pl (run -s to print the
  minimal DB role it needs; -h for options).

Upgrading the database to 1.51 (audit log)
------------------------------------------

Version 1.51 adds an append-only audit_log table (plus a small app_state table).
Run the idempotent, PostgreSQL 8.2-compatible script once:

    psql -U fetchconfig -d fetchconfig -f fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.51.sql

It is safe to re-run, and the same changes are also included in fetchconfig-web-
dbupdate-1.50.sql. The web user is granted INSERT + SELECT on audit_log only (it
can never alter or erase the log); a DBA handles any retention/pruning.

Upgrading the database to 1.50
------------------------------

Version 1.50 adds per-site device access, which needs two new tables (sites,
user_sites) and one new column (users.download_full_report). Run the idempotent
upgrade script once against the fetchconfig-web database:

    psql -U fetchconfig -d fetchconfig -f fetchconfig-web-dbupdate-1.50.sql

It is safe to re-run. It:

- creates sites and user_sites and the download_full_report column if missing;
- seeds the reserved * "All sites" entry (id 0);
- gives every existing user site 0, so everyone starts unrestricted and nothing
  changes for them until you narrow their sites;
- grants the web database user the privileges it needs on the new tables (this
  matters if you run the script as postgres rather than as the web user).

Upgrading from a version before 1.15 also needs the admin_function column; see
"The user database" in README.md.

New configuration keys in 1.50
------------------------------

Both are optional and default off; add them if you want the behaviour:

  Key: HTTPS_ENABLED
  Default: 0
  Effect: 1 adds the Secure flag to the session cookie. Set it once you serve
          over HTTPS (Step 7).

  Key: SHOW_RENDER_TIME
  Default: 0
  Effect: 1 shows the page load/render-time lines (device list, status, log,
          template list, editor render and per-card AJAX times). A diagnostic
          aid.

Part 4 -- Verification and troubleshooting
==========================================

Checklist
---------

Work through this after a fresh install or an upgrade:

- [ ] perl -c /www/cgi-bin/fetchconfig-web.cgi reports syntax OK (proves the
  Perl modules are present).
- [ ] The login page loads over HTTPS and shows the backdrop image.
- [ ] You can log in as admin and the default-password warning disappears after
  you change it.
- [ ] Devices lists your devices with the correct Site column.
- [ ] Devices -> Device backup status and Backup reports open.
- [ ] Setup devices -> Edit device table opens, a device expands on Edit, and
  Save changes writes a .bak into BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE.
- [ ] Tools -> Check site assignment reports no untagged devices (or lists the
  ones to fix).
- [ ] Backup Now on one device completes and the new backup appears in the
  repository.
- [ ] Under mod_fcgid: Tools -> Report logo accepts a logo close to the 100 KB
  cap, and Templates -> Upload HEX capture accepts a capture of a few hundred
  KB. A bare 500 with exceeds MaxRequestLen in the error log means
  FcgidMaxRequestLen is still at its 128 KB default -- see Step 7.3.
- [ ] The footer shows the expected version: fetchconfig-web v2.07 using
  fetchconfig v9.6x.

Common problems
---------------

"Internal Server Error" on first request. Almost always a missing Perl module or
a non-executable script. Run perl -c fetchconfig-web.cgi as the web user, check
chmod 755, and read the web server's error log.

Login succeeds but immediately returns to the login page. The session cookie is
not being accepted. Either SESSION_DIR is not writable by the web user, or
HTTPS_ENABLED = 1 is set on an HTTP-only site (the browser then discards the
Secure cookie). Check SESSION_DIR permissions and the HTTPS_ENABLED value.

"Configuration error" on the login page. One of the URL-path keys (BASE_URL,
CSS_BASE_URL, FONT_BASE_URL, IMAGE_BASE_URL, HELP_BASE_URL) is not an absolute
URL path, or a required key is missing. The message names the key.

The editor opens but Save fails with "could not open ... for writing". The
device table file is not writable by the web user (Step 5). Only the file needs
to be writable, not its directory.

Save fails with "The device table changed on disk since you opened the editor".
Someone else saved, or a cron job rewrote the table, between your open and your
save. Nothing was written. Reload the editor and redo the change -- this guard
is what stops two people overwriting each other.

Backup Now ends with a blank or truncated page; the Apache error log shows
(70007)The timeout specified has expired: ap_content_length_filter:
apr_bucket_read() failed. The web server saw no output for its Timeout. Since
1.60 the keepalive (every 10 s) prevents this; on older releases raise Timeout
above BACKUP_TIMEOUT. If it still occurs, a reverse proxy or load balancer in
front is buffering the response (see the warning under Requirements).

Backup Now shows "a password is required" or similar sudo text. The sudoers rule
from Step 6 is not matching: check the exact web-server user and the exact path
to fetchconfig.pl, and that the file in /etc/sudoers.d/ has mode 0440.

Everything looks right but a recent fix is "not there". Hard-reload the browser
(Ctrl-F5); the editor's JavaScript is cached.

Running the test suite
======================

The package ships its regression tests. They run without a database or web
server (the test harness stubs both) and take under a minute:

    cd fetchconfig-web-<version>
    prove -I t/lib t/

or, via the included Makefile.PL:

    perl Makefile.PL && make test

All tests should pass. Failures almost always mean a missing Perl module on the
build host.
